<style>
 #mol-{{ section.id }} { background: var(--paper); color: var(--ink); }
 #mol-{{ section.id }} .g { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: clamp(32px, 6vw, 80px); align-items: center; }
 #mol-{{ section.id }} .vis { max-width: 460px; width: 100%; margin: 0 auto; color: var(--ink); }
 #mol-{{ section.id }} h2 { margin: 20px 0 0; }
 #mol-{{ section.id }} .statement { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: clamp(20px, 1.8vw, 24px); line-height: 1.6; color: rgba(11,31,58,.85); margin: 28px 0 0; }
 #mol-{{ section.id }} .statement strong { font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); }
 @media (max-width: 749px) { #mol-{{ section.id }} .g { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 36px; } }
</style>
<section id="mol-{{ section.id }}" class="ip6 ip6-section ip6-reveal">
 <div class="ip6-wrap g">
 <div class="vis ip6-rise d1">{% render 'ip6-molecule', size: '100%', animate: true %}</div>
 <div class="ip6-rise d2">
 <span class="ip6-eyebrow">First, the molecule</span>
 <h2 class="ip6-h2">IP6 is a molecule, not a marketing term.</h2>
 <p class="statement">IP6 is <strong>inositol hexaphosphate</strong>, a molecule found naturally in seeds, grains, and legumes, where it stores energy and minerals. IP6 Original pairs it with <strong>Inositol</strong>, the simpler compound at its core, the way Prof. Shamsuddin's research describes the two. Knowing what the molecule is makes the next question, how pure is it, the one that matters.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>
{% schema %}
{ "name": "IP6 Molecule", "tag": "section", "settings": [], "presets": [{ "name": "IP6 Molecule" }] }
{% endschema %}
